claim:
Hangup has three routes:
- an extra completion LLM call after each response (hangup_after_LLMCall, own model via CHECK_FOR_COMPLETION_LLM);
- an end_call tool, either global ('primary' / 'primary_with_shadow_hangup', which also hints at an internal shadow experiment) or node-scoped on graph agents;
- duration and silence caps.
Voicemail detection is still an LLM reading the transcript (default gpt-4.1-mini, every 7 s for up to 30 s, minimum 7 words). That confirms the l4a parity row voicemail_amd, read at 8e300d9, still holds at bc59921.

At bc59921 there is still no cross-call memory and no learning or optimisation loop:
- memory/ holds only a per-call in-memory cache;
- a grep finds 0 hits for previous_call / cross-call / recall;
- there is no A/B or bandit code.
The engine does emit the per-call signals such a loop would need: interruption counts, barge-in recovery, talk-listen time, longest monologue, user-bot latencies, and dead air inside replies. This confirms the l4a memory_across_calls row, read at 8e300d9.

Fixed audio is reused across calls by three mechanisms, none of them the synthesizer cache:
- the backend pre-synthesizes the welcome and hands it to the engine as base64;
- static graph-node clips come from S3;
- language-handoff clips sit in a process-wide cache (max 256).
synthesizer.caching=true is an in-memory cache that lasts one call.

The engine is heavily tested and actively hardened:
- 175 test files with 2,072 test functions;
- HEAD commit 2026-10-20 (BOLNA-2967);
- comments cite incident tickets (BOLNA-2563, BOLNA-2621);
- HTTP/2 is disabled for LLM calls because cancelled h2 streams leaked on barge-in.
Parity with this engine on edge-case handling is real work, not a weekend clone.
